From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 07:57:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] timekeep: match the app where it actually runs

The seapp_contexts entry requires user=system, but the app declares no
sharedUserId, so it runs under its own app uid and the rule never
matches. It falls through to platform_app, which may not set the
vendor_timekeep_prop this policy already grants vendor_timekeep_app --
and SystemProperties.set throws on refusal:

  com.sony.timekeep FATAL: failed to set system property
    "persist.vendor.timeadjust" at TimeKeep receiver

So the receiver dies on every TIME_SET and the save half of timekeep has
never run, while vendor.timekeep-restore keeps restoring an increasingly
stale clock. Matching on user=_app, in the form plat_seapp_contexts uses
for a platform-signed app domain, puts it in the domain whose rules were
written for it.
---
 sepolicy/vendor/seapp_contexts | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sepolicy/vendor/seapp_contexts b/sepolicy/vendor/seapp_contexts
index f0e9bc8..ed765ba 100644
--- a/sepolicy/vendor/seapp_contexts
+++ b/sepolicy/vendor/seapp_contexts
@@ -1 +1,7 @@
-user=system seinfo=platform name=com.sony.timekeep domain=vendor_timekeep_app
+# The app has no sharedUserId, so it runs under its own app uid and user=system never matched:
+# it fell through to platform_app, which may not set the vendor_timekeep_prop this policy
+# already grants vendor_timekeep_app. SystemProperties.set throws on refusal, so the receiver
+# died on every TIME_SET and the save half of timekeep never ran -- while the restore half
+# (vendor.timekeep-restore) happily restored an increasingly stale clock.
+# Matches the form plat_seapp_contexts uses for a platform-signed app domain (traceur_app).
+user=_app seinfo=platform name=com.sony.timekeep domain=vendor_timekeep_app type=app_data_file levelFrom=all
